---
Inspect the names, shapes, and sizes of checkpoint tensors before binding them
to a ZML model. Run the commands from the checkout in the
[ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/quickstart')).

# [Inspect a local checkpoint]($heading.id('inspect-a-local-checkpoint'))

Pass an absolute path to the `safetensors` command in the I/O playground.
Replace the path with the location of your weights.

```sh
bazel run //examples/io:playground -- safetensors \
  "${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM2-350M/model.safetensors"
```

The output groups tensors by name and shows their shapes and byte sizes.
For a checkpoint split across several files, pass its `model.safetensors.index.json`
and keep the referenced shards alongside the index. A checkpoint directory also
works when it contains a [recognized entry point](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/safetensors.zig#L597-L615).

# [Inspect a remote checkpoint]($heading.id('inspect-a-remote-checkpoint'))

Use `ls` to find the weights, then pass the file URI to `safetensors`. This
example selects a fixed revision of
[LiquidAI/LFM2-350M](https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM2-350M/tree/f37d3f5c8c5484bc01dad379a595cf4c68c4e70e).

```sh
checkpoint='hf://LiquidAI/LFM2-350M@f37d3f5c8c5484bc01dad379a595cf4c68c4e70e'
bazel run //examples/io:playground -- ls "$checkpoint"
bazel run //examples/io:playground -- safetensors "$checkpoint/model.safetensors"
```

The inspector reads Safetensors metadata. The `hf://` loader uses `main` when
you omit `@revision`. For gated repositories, request access and set
`HF_TOKEN`. The playground also registers `s3://` and `gs://` for object
storage. See [Environment variables]($link.page('zml/reference/environment').unsafeRef('model-storage-and-credentials'))
for credentials and storage settings.

# [Load weights for execution]($heading.id('load-weights-for-execution'))

To check that weights transfer to the selected backend, pass a small local
checkpoint to the playground's `load` command.

```sh
bazel run //examples/io:playground -- load \
  "${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM2-350M/model.safetensors" replicated
```

`replicated` places a full copy on each logical device. Allow memory for the
copies and transfer buffers. The playground logs bytes loaded and elapsed time,
then releases the buffers. [Load command source](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/examples/io/main.zig#L153-L222)

Run the MNIST example to see ZML load a pretrained checkpoint and classify a
digit. Its Bazel target supplies the weights and sample dataset.

```sh
bazel run //examples/mnist
```

The [MNIST source](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/examples/mnist/mnist.zig#L17-L122)
opens a `TensorRegistry` and creates a `TensorStore` that borrows that registry
and the platform's replicated mesh. The model uses a view with prefix `fc1`
to create symbolic tensors bound to `fc1.weight` and `fc1.bias`. For example,
`store.view().withPrefix("fc1").createReplicatedTensor("weight", .{ .d_out, .d })`
uses the checkpoint shape and tags the weight matrix's two axes.

After compiling `forward`, the example fills a buffer structure that mirrors
the model. Here is the loading sequence inside `Mnist.load`, with `self` as
the model and `store` as a pointer to its tensor store.

```zig
var buffers = try zml.mem.bufferize(allocator, Mnist, self);
errdefer unloadBuffers(&buffers);

var loader: zml.io.Loader = try .init(allocator, platform, .default);
defer loader.deinit();

try loader.load(io, Mnist, self, &buffers, store, .{});
try loader.await(io);
return buffers;
```

Keep the model, store, registry, and platform alive while the transfer is in
flight. After `await`, bind the loaded buffers and the input buffer with
[`args.set`]($link.page('zml/first-model').ref('compile-and-call-the-layer')).
The caller owns the returned `zml.Bufferized(Mnist)` and releases
each weight buffer with `deinit` before releasing the platform.

The walkthrough follows the pinned source. Execution checks are pending.
